\tag{fdip}
\title{Finite gauge fields and an exact torus law}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{A [connection background](fdip-006C) assigns a fixed transport to each
edge. We now sum over these transports as field variables, while keeping
the graph, its square faces and the group fixed. The faces are extra
incidence data: the earlier graph alone does not specify them. A chosen
unitary representation gives a Wilson action and a finite probability
law with an explicit counting-measure convention.}

\p{Gauge averaging first determines which parts of an observable can
contribute to its expectation. For the two-element group on a square
torus, all plaquette signs can then be counted exactly. One global
constraint and two remaining cycle holonomies account for the partition
function's normalization. The resulting loop formula gives a pure-gauge
area law in two dimensions; no matter field is included in that result.}

